Order-dependent tests: they pass alone, fail together (or the reverse). Save /workspace/featureflags.py:

_flags = {}

def enable(name):
    _flags[name] = True

def is_enabled(name):
    return _flags.get(name, False)

def reset():
    _flags.clear()

and /workspace/tests/test_a_admin.py:

import featureflags

def test_admin_panel_visible_when_enabled():
    featureflags.enable("admin_panel")
    assert featureflags.is_enabled("admin_panel")

and /workspace/tests/test_b_guest.py:

import featureflags

def test_guest_sees_no_admin_panel():
    assert not featureflags.is_enabled("admin_panel")

Reproduce both orders: pytest tests/test_a_admin.py tests/test_b_guest.py fails the guest test, while pytest tests/test_b_guest.py tests/test_a_admin.py passes; also show pytest -p no:randomly default collection order. Diagnose in a short comment: shared module state leaks between tests. Fix it structurally, not by reordering: an autouse fixture in tests/conftest.py that calls featureflags.reset() around each test (yield-style, reset before AND after), and prove order-independence by running both orders plus pytest --lf and 5 repeated full runs, all green. Bonus hardening: install pytest-randomly and run twice with different seeds, still green.

